Presidential elections were held in [[Madagascar]] on 3 November 1996, with a second round on 29 December 1996.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://africanelections.tripod.com/mg.html#1996_Presidential_Election|title=Elections in Madagascar|website=African Elections Database|date=15 November 2011 |access-date=8 April 2026|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260417043926/https://africanelections.tripod.com/mg.html#1996_Presidential_Election|archive-date=17 Apr 2026|url-status=dead}}</ref> The election followed the [[impeachment]] of incumbent [[List of Presidents of Madagascar|President]] [[Albert Zafy]] and the appointment of his interim successor, [[Norbert Ratsirahonana]]. [[Didier Ratsiraka]] won a narrow victory after a runoff.

==Background==
Madagascar became independent from France in 1960, forming the [[First Republic of Madagascar]]. After student-led protests in 1972, then-President [[Philibert Tsiranana]] turned over control to General [[Gabriel Ramanantsoa]]. In 1975, [[Didier Ratsiraka]] was named as President, and confirmed by a referendum. This began the Second Republic of Madagascar. Ratsiraka held a tight grip on power until 1991, when protests led by [[Albert Zafy]] led to the [[Panorama Convention]], beginning a governmental transition. A 1992 constitutional referendum established the Third Republic, with its first presidential election in 1993.<ref name="Marcus2004">{{cite web |last1=Marcus |first1=Richard |title=Political Change in Madagascar: Populist Democracy or Neopatrimonialism by Another Name? – Africa Portal |url=https://africaportal.org/publication/political-change-in-madagascar-populist-democracy-or-neopatrimonialism-by-another-name/ |website=Africa Portal |access-date=8 April 2026 |date=1 August 2004}}</ref>{{rp|2}}

Ratsiraka was unpopular by the time the 1993 election came, in part because he had been recorded ordering the presidential guard to shoot unarmed protestors during the 1991 unrest.<ref name="Gow1997">{{cite journal |last1=Gow |first1=Bonar A. |title=Admiral Didier Ratsiraka and the Malagasy Socialist Revolution |journal=The Journal of Modern African Studies |date=1997 |volume=35 |issue=3 |pages=409–439 |url=https://www.jstor.org/stable/161749 |access-date=9 April 2026 |issn=0022-278X}}</ref>{{rp|435}} Zafy defeated Ratsiraka in the [[1992–93 Malagasy presidential election|1993 election]] handily, 67% to 33%.<ref name="Herbert2002">{{cite book |last1=Herbert |first1=Ross |title=Madagascar: Avoidable disaster, test case for African diplomacy |date=1 January 2002 |publisher=South African Institute for International Affairs |url=https://africaportal.org/publication/madagascar-avoidable-disaster-test-case-for-african-diplomacy/ |access-date=9 April 2026|isbn=9781919810553}}</ref>{{rp|7}} Once in power, Zafy chafed at the constraints the new constitution placed on the president. In 1995, he successfully spearheaded a [[1995 Malagasy constitutional referendum|referendum to amend the constitution]] to centralize power in the presidency. However, the [[National Assembly (Madagascar)|National Assembly]] objected to his centralization of power, and in July 1996, they impeached Zafy. The [[High Constitutional Court of Madagascar|High Constitutional Court]] ratified the impeachment in September, but Zafy was allowed to run in the election scheduled for November 1996.<ref name=Marcus2004/>{{rp|2-3}}<ref>{{cite web |title=Malagasy president impeached |url=https://mg.co.za/article/1996-09-20-malagasy-president-impeached/ |website=The Mail & Guardian |access-date=9 April 2026 |language=en-ZA |date=20 September 1996}}</ref>

==Candidates==
Three incumbent or former Presidents ran in this election: Ratsiraka, Zafy, and [[Norbert Ratsirahonana]], who was named acting President after Zafy was impeached.<ref name="MR2003">{{cite journal |last1=Marcus |first1=Richard R. |last2=Razafindrakoto |first2=Paul |title=Participation and the Poverty of Electoral Democracy in Madagascar |journal=Africa Spectrum |date=2003 |volume=38 |issue=1 |pages=27–48 |url=https://www.jstor.org/stable/40174961 |access-date=6 April 2026 |issn=0002-0397}}</ref>{{rp|29}} 
The fourth candidate with a significant voter base was [[Herizo Razafimahaleo]], the former Minister for Industrial Promotion.<ref name=Marcus2004/>{{rp|3}}

==Election==
{{Citations broken|section|date=April 2026}}
In the first round, former President Didier Ratsiraka took first place with 36.6% of the vote with a turnout of 58.41%.<ref>{{Cite web |date=June 2010 |title=Madagascar: 1996 Presidential election results |url=http://www.eisa.org.za/WEP/mad1996results.htm |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100902222022/http://www.eisa.org.za/WEP/mad1996results.htm |archive-date=2 Sep 2010 |access-date=8 Oct 2026 |website=African Democracy Encyclopaedia Project}}</ref> However, as he failed to win more than 50% of the vote, a second round was held, in which Zafy, who had been able to run despite his impeachment, was the sole alternative.<ref name="Allen">{{Cite book |title=Checking executive power: presidential impeachment in comparative perspective |date=2003 |publisher=Praeger |isbn=978-0-313-05183-8 |editor-last=Baumgartner |editor-first=Jody C. |location=Westport, Conn |pages=91 |doi=10.5040/9798216187639 |editor-last2=Kada |editor-first2=Naoko}}</ref>

[[Economic Liberalism and Democratic Action for National Recovery|LEADER-Fanilo]] candidate [[Herizo Razafimahaleo]], who took third place with 15.1%, backed Ratsiraka for the second round.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Mahery |url=http://www.herizo.org/spip.php?article24 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20071008081430/http://www.herizo.org/spip.php?article24 |archive-date=2007-10-08 |access-date=2026-10-08 |website=www.herizo.org |language=fr}}</ref> Ratsirahonana, the acting President and Prime Minister, who stood as a candidate and received fourth place with 10.1% of the vote,<ref>{{Cite web |title=Rapport de la Mission d'Observation de l'Élection Présidentielle du 29 décembre 1996 (2è tour) |url=http://democratie.francophonie.org/IMG/pdf/Presidentielles_du_29_decembre_1996.pdf |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160304022755/http://democratie.francophonie.org/IMG/pdf/Presidentielles_du_29_decembre_1996.pdf |archive-date=2016-03-04}}</ref> backed Zafy.<ref name=Allen/>

In the second round, Ratsiraka achieved a narrow victory. On 6 January 1997, Zafy alleged that vote rigging and irregularities had occurred.<ref>"Madagascar: Zafy alleges vote rigging, "irregularities" in presidential final ballot", Malagasy National Radio, Antananarivo, 6 January 1997</ref> Final results confirming Ratsiraka's victory were announced by the High Constitutional Court on 31 January,<ref>"Madagascar: Didier Ratsiraka wins presidential election by small margin", AFP, 31 January 1997</ref> and he was sworn in on 9 February.<ref>"Ratsiraka sworn in as Madagascar's new president", Television Malagasy (Antananarivo), 9 February 1997</ref> Zafy conceded defeat in early 1997.<ref name="Bratton1998">{{cite journal |last1=Bratton |first1=Michael |title=Second Elections in Africa |journal=Journal of Democracy |date=July 1998 |volume=9 |issue=3 |pages=51–66 |doi=10.1353/jod.1998.0041 |url=https://muse.jhu.edu/pub/1/article/16894/summary |access-date=10 April 2026|url-access=subscription }}</ref>{{rp|61}}

